          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- BASELINE COMPARISON PANEL -->
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-title">The Hidden 0.5 °C — Baseline Comparison</div>
        <div class="baseline-compare">
          <div class="compare-side ipcc">
            <div class="compare-tag">IPCC / AR6 Reported</div>
            <div class="compare-temp">~1.2°C</div>
            <div class="compare-baseline">Baseline: 1850–1900</div>
            <div class="compare-note">Conventional instrumental start; already contained significant early industrial warming</div>
          </div>
          <div class="compare-side true-pi">
            <div class="compare-tag">True Pre-Industrial</div>
            <div class="compare-temp">&gt;1.5°C</div>
            <div class="compare-baseline">Baseline: 1700–1790</div>
            <div class="compare-note">Sclerosponge reconstruction reveals total warming from genuinely pre-industrial conditions</div>
          </div>
          <div class="hidden-warming-callout">
            <div class="hidden-warming-arrow">+0.5°C</div>
            <div class="hidden-warming-text">
              <strong>~0.5 °C of warming is "hidden"</strong> because it occurred before 1850.
              The 1850–1900 period, used as the IPCC zero point, was already ~0.5 °C above the true
              pre-industrial mean — meaning the Paris Agreement's 1.5 °C threshold has effectively already been crossed.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div><!-- /left-col -->

    <!-- RIGHT COLUMN -->
    <div class="right-col">

      <!-- WHY SCLEROSPONGES -->
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-title">Why Sclerosponges?</div>
        <div class="feature-list">
          <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-dot"></div>
            <div class="feature-text"><strong>Sr/Ca proxy:</strong> strontium-to-calcium ratios in aragonite skeletons faithfully track sea-surface temperature at annual resolution</div>
          </div>
          <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-dot"></div>
            <div class="feature-text"><strong>Continuous growth</strong> over centuries yields unbroken records back to ~1700 CE — 150 years before the instrumental era</div>
          </div>
          <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-dot"></div>
            <div class="feature-text"><strong>Caribbean sites</strong> (33–91 m depth) capture large-scale tropical SST variability representative of global mean temperature</div>
          </div>
          <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-dot"></div>
            <div class="feature-text"><strong>Independent proxy:</strong> no overlap with institutional weather networks that began coverage only ~1850</div>
          </div>
          <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-dot"></div>
            <div class="feature-text"><strong>Validated r²&gt;0.9</strong> against HadCRUT5 and NOAA ERSSTv5 over their overlapping 1960–present intervals</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TEMPERATURE TIMELINE -->
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-title">Key Milestones</div>
        <div class="timeline-list">
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
            <div class="timeline-period">1700–1790</div>
            <div class="timeline-obs">Stable pre-industrial temperatures; defined as the true baseline reference</div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
            <div class="timeline-period">1790–1850</div>
            <div class="timeline-obs">Onset of detectable warming linked to early industrialisation; +0.2–0.4 °C rise</div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
            <div class="timeline-period">1850–1900</div>
            <div class="timeline-obs">Already ~0.5 °C above 1700–1790 mean — the hidden warming already baked in</div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
            <div class="timeline-period">1900–1960</div>
            <div class="timeline-obs">Gradual warming continues; volcanic cooling episodes visible in proxy record</div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
            <div class="timeline-period">1960–2000</div>
            <div class="timeline-obs">Accelerating trend; sclerosponge and instrumental records converge (r²&gt;0.9)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
            <div class="timeline-period">2000–present</div>
            <div class="timeline-obs" style="color:#f87171;font-weight:600;">Warming exceeds 1.5 °C relative to the true pre-industrial baseline (~2010)</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- POLICY IMPLICATIONS -->
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-title">Policy Implications</div>
        <div class="policy-grid">
          <div class="policy-item urgent">
            <strong>Remaining carbon budget for 1.5 °C is effectively zero</strong> — adopting the true pre-industrial baseline eliminates the remaining allowance
          </div>
          <div class="policy-item">
            The <strong>2.0 °C guardrail</strong> is also closer: only ~0.3 °C remaining, not ~0.8 °C as implied by IPCC baseline
          </div>
          <div class="policy-item">
            IPCC operationally uses <strong>1850–1900</strong> as "pre-industrial," but this period already reflected early industrial emissions
          </div>
          <div class="policy-item urgent">
            Findings underscore urgency of <strong>rapid emissions reductions</strong> and transparent communication on how baselines affect perceived targets
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

